From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenMANET Pi 5 port <noreply@openmanet.org>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:30:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] openmanetd: recognise bcm2712,mm6108-spi as a supported board

Adds a package patch to the openmanetd feed package, the same way
0002-APP-4789-collectd adds one to collectd.

openmanetd reads /etc/board.json and gates three capabilities on
board.Model.ID in internal/util/board/supported_features.go:
GNSSsupoorted(), BLOSsupported() and CommsSupported(). Every one of those
switch statements ends in `default: return false`, and internal/util/board/
board_type.go has no bcm2712 constant. The Pi 5 product reports
"bcm2712,mm6108-spi", so it falls to the default branch and all three
features are silently reported unsupported.

That is wrong on this hardware: the Pi 5 product carries the same Seeed
WM1302 HAT and the same Morse Micro MM6108 over SPI as bcm2711,mm6108-spi -
including the same Quectel GPS on the HAT - so the capability answers must
match the Pi 4 SPI board exactly. The patch adds BCM2712_MM6108_SPI and
lists it beside BCM2711_MM6108_SPI in all three switches.

This is NOT related to the missing batman-adv interface, which is
operator-provisioned state (see PI5_PORT_STATUS.md). It is a separate port
gap found while tracing that question, and it is what would have made the
WM1302 GPS look broken later for a non-obvious reason.

Only the bcm2712,mm6108-spi variant is added - the SDIO and MM8108 Pi 5
variants are not built by this product, and listing boards we do not ship
would be a claim we have not tested.

Board-scoped to ekh-bcm2712, so the shipping Pi 4 product builds openmanetd
from unmodified upstream source.

--- /dev/null
+++ b/feeds/openmanet/openmanetd/patches/001-board-add-bcm2712-mm6108-spi.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+From: OpenMANET Pi 5 port <noreply@openmanet.org>
+Subject: [PATCH] board: recognise bcm2712,mm6108-spi
+
+openmanetd gates GNSS, BLOS and Comms on board.Model.ID read from
+/etc/board.json, and every one of those switches ends in `default: return
+false`. The Raspberry Pi 5 product reports "bcm2712,mm6108-spi", which is
+absent from the board_type constants, so all three features are silently
+reported unsupported on hardware that does support them.
+
+The Pi 5 product is the same Seeed WM1302 HAT and the same Morse Micro
+MM6108 over SPI as bcm2711,mm6108-spi - only the host SoC differs - so the
+capability answers must be identical. Add the constant and list it alongside
+BCM2711_MM6108_SPI in all three switches.
+
+ExecutionProfileFor() is deliberately left alone: unlisted boards get the
+zero profile, which is the correct "no runtime override" answer for a
+4-core Cortex-A76.
+--- a/internal/util/board/board_type.go
++++ b/internal/util/board/board_type.go
+@@ -6,6 +6,9 @@
+ 	BCM2711_MM6108_SDIO string = "bcm2711,mm6108-sdio"
+ 	BCM2711_MM8108_USB  string = "bcm2711,mm8108-usb"
+ 
++	// BCM2712
++	BCM2712_MM6108_SPI string = "bcm2712,mm6108-spi"
++
+ 	// BCM2710
+ 	BCM2710_MM6108_SPI  string = "bcm2710,mm6108-spi"
+ 	BCM2710_MM6108_SDIO string = "bcm2710,mm6108-sdio"
+--- a/internal/util/board/supported_features.go
++++ b/internal/util/board/supported_features.go
+@@ -35,7 +35,8 @@
+ 	switch boardConfigInfo.Model.ID {
+ 	case BCM2711_MM6108_SPI,
+ 		BCM2711_MM6108_SDIO,
+-		BCM2711_MM8108_USB:
++		BCM2711_MM8108_USB,
++		BCM2712_MM6108_SPI:
+ 		return true
+ 	case BCM2710_MM6108_SPI, BCM2710_MM6108_SDIO:
+ 		return true
+@@ -81,7 +82,8 @@
+ 	switch boardConfigInfo.Model.ID {
+ 	case BCM2711_MM6108_SPI,
+ 		BCM2711_MM6108_SDIO,
+-		BCM2711_MM8108_USB:
++		BCM2711_MM8108_USB,
++		BCM2712_MM6108_SPI:
+ 		return true
+ 	case BCM2710_MM6108_SPI, BCM2710_MM6108_SDIO:
+ 		return false
+@@ -133,6 +135,7 @@
+ 	case BCM2711_MM6108_SPI,
+ 		BCM2711_MM6108_SDIO,
+ 		BCM2711_MM8108_USB,
++		BCM2712_MM6108_SPI,
+ 		BCM2710_MM6108_SPI,
+ 		BCM2710_MM6108_SDIO,
+ 		HalowLink2,
